From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: The Playport authors <dev@playport.dev>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 16:53:28 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] ARM64EC: calls into ARM64EC code return to the JIT with RET
 on iOS

A guest CALL into ARM64EC code leaves the JIT for ExitFunctionEC; the
callee returns through __os_arm64x_dispatch_ret, enter_jit and the
dispatcher's EnterEC entry, which on iOS branches to the loop top: EC
code bitmap check, trap flag check, L1 probe, then `br` to the block.
Calls through the import table reach the dispatcher with `blr` and push
a return-address predictor entry nothing pops, so the caller's own RET,
and each RET above it, is predicted from another call's entry.

With IosEcReturnWithRet set (read when the dispatcher and blocks are
emitted):
- the direct CALL path into ARM64EC code leaves with `blr` too
  (ExitFunctionEC loads LR itself on every path that uses it), so every
  guest CALL into ARM64EC code pushes one entry; JMPs keep `br`;
- EnterEC probes L1 itself and enters a hit with `ret`, popping it. The
  trap flag, a miss and an empty entry take the loop top as before.
An L1 entry exists only for an address the loop top already found
outside the EC code bitmap, so skipping that test cannot change the
outcome. The returned-to path drops from 22 instructions to 10.

Madeira took this after eliding the call-ret shadow stack (its
813bc99); Playport keeps that stack (fex 0006), and this does not touch
it: the EC call path never pushed a call-ret entry, and EnterEC on iOS
never popped one, with the switch on or off.

On by default, as in Madeira; MADEIRA_FEX_EC_RET=0 at launch keeps `br`
and the loop-top re-entry, byte for byte. One "FEX: ec-ret-v1 ...
enabled=" line logs the choice.

Madeira-commit: 1e771919a65bf1a344304ad03b9bd7649a1221e5 (willfaust/FEX)
Linear: PLA-364
Class: feature
Evidence: docs/evidence/2026-10-20-rdr2-perf-ports.md
Offered-upstream: no; the iOS EnterEC path is the iOS port's
